{"causes": ["The Neolithic Revolution necessitated the domestication of plants and animals for stable food production.", "The human need for food preservation led to the discovery and application of fermentation processes.", "Empirical observation of natural biological processes, such as decay and growth, prompted early experimentation."], "effects": ["Biotechnology led to increased food security, supporting significant population growth and settled communities.", "The development of agricultural societies became possible, fostering urbanization and specialized labor.", "Early biotechnological practices laid the empirical foundation for future scientific understanding of biological mechanisms."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "neolithic-revolution", "sourceName": "Neolithic Revolution", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "biotechnology", "targetName": "biotechnology", "context": "The shift to settled agriculture during the Neolithic Revolution around 10,000 BCE created the impetus for early biotechnological practices like selective breeding and fermentation."}, {"sourceSlug": "biotechnology", "sourceName": "biotechnology", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "fermentation", "targetName": "Fermentation", "context": "Ancient biotechnology developed and refined fermentation processes for food and drink, evident in Sumerian beer brewing by 4000 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "biotechnology", "sourceName": "biotechnology", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-egypt", "targetName": "Ancient Egypt", "context": "Early biotechnological practices, particularly bread making and brewing, were central to the economy and daily life in Ancient Egypt from around 3100 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "biotechnology", "sourceName": "biotechnology", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "agriculture", "targetName": "Agriculture", "context": "Classical biotechnology, through selective breeding and crop rotation, profoundly influenced the productivity and evolution of agriculture across the ancient world."}, {"sourceSlug": "selective-breeding", "sourceName": "Selective Breeding", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "biotechnology", "targetName": "biotechnology", "context": "Selective breeding of plants and animals, practiced for millennia, represents a fundamental application of classical biotechnology to improve species characteristics."}], "places": [{"name": "Fertile Crescent, Middle East", "role": "Origin of early agricultural biotechnology"}, {"name": "Ancient Egypt, Africa", "role": "Center for brewing and bread making"}, {"name": "Indus Valley, Asia", "role": "Site of early crop domestication"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: biotechnology", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "biotechnology"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7108", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biotechnology"], "tags": ["academic discipline", "Technological Innovations", "scientific-paradigm"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "biotechnology left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 132 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Influenced intellectual traditions and cultural development.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 9, "significanceNarrative": "Classical biotechnology, encompassing practices like selective breeding and fermentation, fundamentally transformed human societies by enabling stable food production and preservation, directly leading to urbanization, population growth, and the rise of complex civilizations across continents.", "significanceCategory": "world-changing"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:13:37Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30885978740", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Technologies directly involving biology; formerly or more narrowly the use of living systems and organisms to develop or make useful products", "newValue": "Biotechnology, in its classical instantiation, refers to the ancient and empirical manipulation of living organisms or their components to create useful products or processes, fundamentally shaping human civilization long before the term's modern coinage. Originating with the Neolithic Revolution around 10,000 BCE, these rudimentary biological technologies were critical for transitioning from nomadic hunter-gatherer societies to settled agricultural communities, providing stable food sources, preserving harvests, and improving nutrition across diverse regions from Mesopotamia to ancient China.

Key achievements of classical biotechnology include the mastery of fermentation for brewing beer and winemaking in Sumeria and Egypt by 4000 BCE, along with the leavening of bread and production of cheese, all relying on microbial action. Selective breeding of crops like wheat and barley, and animals such as cattle and sheep, dramatically enhanced agricultural productivity and domesticated species characteristics over millennia. These early applications were driven by necessity and observation, with techniques refined through generations of practical experience rather than scientific understanding, yet they laid the groundwork for complex societies.

The legacy of classical biotechnology is profound, underpinning the rise of empires, enabling significant population growth, and fostering the development of specialized crafts and trade. These foundational practices provided the caloric surplus and food security necessary for urban development, the division of labor, and the emergence of intellectual pursuits in Greece and Rome. The empirical knowledge accumulated over these eras formed an essential, if unarticulated, scientific heritage that would eventually inform the biological revolutions of later centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:13:37Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30885978740", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Earlier philosophical traditions in the 3000 BCE – 500 CE", "type": "Idea", "year": "3000 BCE"}, {"title": "Empirical observations in the 3000 BCE – 500 CE", "type": "Idea", "year": "3000 BCE"}, {"title": "Cultural context in the 3000 BCE – 500 CE", "type": "Idea", "year": "3000 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The Neolithic Revolution necessitated the domestication of plants and animals for stable food production.", "The human need for food preservation led to the discovery and application of fermentation processes.", "Empirical observation of natural biological processes, such as decay and growth, prompted early experimentation."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-04T07:13:37Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30885978740", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Influenced later thought through biotechnology's historical significance", "type": "Idea"}, {"title": "Shaped institutions through biotechnology's historical significance", "type": "Idea"}, {"title": "Transformed practice through biotechnology's historical significance", "type": "Idea"}], "newValue": ["Biotechnology led to increased food security, supporting significant population growth and settled communities.", "The development of agricultural societies became possible, fostering urbanization a